build/
# Anchored, because it used to be `dist/` and that matched every dist anywhere -
# including the window's, which go:embed needs to EXIST on a tree nobody has
# built. Git cannot re-include a file inside an excluded directory, so the
# directory has to stay visible and its contents get excluded instead.
/dist/
cmd/rigwindow/dist/*
!cmd/rigwindow/dist/.gitkeep

# design/kit is the source and each fake application's kit/ is where the build
# copies it, because go:embed cannot reach above its own package. Same
# arrangement as dist above, and each program's embed_test.go is what notices
# if it breaks. One pair of lines per adopter rather than a cmd/*/kit glob: a
# glob would silently cover a directory somebody added for another reason.
cmd/ledger/kit/*
!cmd/ledger/kit/.gitkeep
cmd/docket/kit/*
!cmd/docket/kit/.gitkeep
node_modules/
*.out
*.test
.air.toml
frontend/.svelte-kit/
ipcbench/bench

# A binary built WITHOUT -o lands in the repo root under its package name, and
# the Makefile's own targets all write to build/ - so anything here is an ad-hoc
# `go build ./cmd/<x>/`. It cost real attention on 2026-09-11: a stray `rig` sat
# untracked beside three seats' working files, visible to every peer's
# git status, in a tree where the hazard of record is a mis-staged commit.
#
# Listed one per adopter rather than as a glob, for the same reason the kit
# directories above are: a glob would silently cover a root file somebody added
# for another reason. Anchored, so only the repo root is affected.
/rig
/rigd
/fakeapp
/ledger
/docket
/ipcbench
/sizeratchet
/depscheck
/footprint
/pkgdeb
/schemagen
# Gitignored symlinks into the logbook. Either path opens the same file; a
# fresh clone remakes them with logbook/SETUP.md. See logbook/CONVENTION.md.
HANDOFF.md
READINESS.txt
BACKLOG.md
DECISIONS.md
